The only remaining bare `composeRefs` in render is navigation-menu.tsx:1081. Let me evaluate this one.

The code:
```tsx
{Array.from(viewportContentContext.items).map(([value, { ref, forceMount, ...props }]) => {
  const isActive = activeContentValue === value;
  return (
    <Presence key={value} present={forceMount || isActive}>
      <NavigationMenuContentImpl
        {...props}
        ref={composeRefs(ref, (node) => {
          if (isActive && node) setContent(node);
        })}
      />
    </Presence>
  );
})}
```

`ref` is a stable ref object from the context items map. The inline arrow is recreated each render. `setContent` is a state setter. `isActive` per item.

Does this loop? On a re-render, the composed ref identity changes → React detaches (calls with null → guard `isActive && null` → falsy → no setContent) and attaches (calls with node → `isActive && node` → setContent(node) → state set to the SAME node → React bails out of re-render). So it converges; it doesn't loop. It's the same underlying instability pattern but it does NOT produce the "Maximum update depth exceeded" bug because of the `isActive &&` guard and idempotent setContent.

Per user instruction "fix only if it is the same bug" — this one is guarded and converges, so it's not the same bug (no infinite loop). I'll report it but not change it.

